        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#deck-type" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. First: What Kind of Deck Do You Have?</a>
          <a href="#why-silicone" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Why Silicone and Nothing Else</a>
          <a href="#bottle-vs-aerosol" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Bottle vs. Aerosol: The Real Decision</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. What to Buy</a>
          <a href="#how-much" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. How Much to Apply</a>
          <a href="#how-often" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. How Often to Apply</a>
          <a href="#signs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Signs You Have Left It Too Long</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. What It Costs Per Year</a>
          <a href="#what-not-to-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. What Not to Buy</a>
          <a href="#while-belt-loose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. While You Have the Belt Loose, Do These Too</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          There is less choice here than the shelf suggests. For the overwhelming majority of home
          treadmills the answer is 100% silicone, and the interesting decisions are how you apply
          it and whether your particular deck should receive it at all.
        </p>

        <div id="deck-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. First: What Kind of Deck Do You Have?</h2>
<p>
            Three deck types exist and they want different things, so this determines everything
            that follows.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Standard decks</strong> are the overwhelming majority of home treadmills. A
            board with a low-friction laminate, needing periodic silicone application. If your
            manual mentions lubricating the belt, or came with a small bottle of clear liquid, you
            have one of these.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pre-waxed decks</strong> arrive with a wax impregnated into the deck surface
            that releases gradually under heat and friction. These must never receive silicone —
            the two do not coexist, and the result is a contaminated surface that has to be
            replaced rather than cleaned. Some are genuinely maintenance-free for their life; some
            take a proprietary wax stick or paraffin-based product.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Self-lubricating decks</strong> use an impregnated low-friction layer, and the
            manufacturer will usually say plainly that no lubrication is required.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you cannot find the manual, search the model number plus "lubrication" on the
            manufacturer's site, or call their support line and ask directly. Do not guess, and do
            not assume from the price or the brand — the same manufacturer often uses different
            deck types across its range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-silicone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Why Silicone and Nothing Else</h2>
<p>
            Silicone is chemically inert with respect to everything it touches on a treadmill. It
            does not attack the deck laminate, it does not degrade the belt's fabric backing, and
            it does not soften the adhesive bonding the belt's layers together. Petroleum-based
            products do all three to varying degrees.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also stays slick rather than becoming tacky. That distinction matters more than any
            other property: a lubricant that turns sticky collects dust, carpet fibre and belt
            debris into an abrasive paste, at which point you have made the friction problem worse
            than before you started. This is exactly what happens with WD-40, cooking oil and
            petroleum jelly, and our
            <a href="/can-you-use-wd40-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to WD-40 on treadmills</a>
            covers that failure in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, silicone has a wide temperature tolerance. A deck under a running belt gets
            genuinely warm, and a lubricant that thins out and migrates away as it heats is not
            doing its job at the moment it is most needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            The specification to look for is <strong>100% silicone</strong>, with no petroleum
            distillates and no added solvents. Products sold explicitly as treadmill lubricant are
            formulated to that standard; general-purpose silicone sprays frequently are not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bottle-vs-aerosol" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Bottle vs. Aerosol: The Real Decision</h2>
<p>
            This is where the choice genuinely matters, and we come down firmly on one side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Squeeze bottles with a long applicator tube</strong> let you place lubricant
            directly under the belt, along the centre line of the deck where it is needed. You use
            very little, none of it goes anywhere it should not, and you can feel the tube against
            the deck so you know where the product is landing. A typical bottle covers a year or
            more of applications.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aerosols</strong> are quicker and worse. Spray drifts, and a treadmill's motor
            compartment — containing the motor, controller board and mains wiring — sits directly
            under the front of the deck. Misting silicone across the deck also puts it on the belt's
            top surface, which is a running surface you do not want slippery, and on the frame and
            floor. Aerosols also use several times as much product per application.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is one case for an aerosol with a precision straw: a deck too tight to slide a
            bottle tube under. Even then, unplug the machine, keep the straw under the belt, and
            use short bursts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What to Buy</h2>
<p>
            These are product categories rather than specific brands, because availability varies
            and the formulations within each category are close to interchangeable. Buy on
            applicator type and the "100% silicone" claim rather than on brand.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
