                        Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
                      </a>
                      {product.reviewUrl && (
                        <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline text-sm hover:underline">
                          Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Much to Apply</h2>
<p>
            Under-applying is common and harmless; over-applying is common and creates its own
            problems. Roughly one ounce — about two tablespoons — is right for a standard home
            treadmill deck, spread along the centre third of the deck under the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            The centre third is where almost all the contact happens. Lubricant applied near the
            rollers migrates to the edges, does little useful work and can find its way onto the
            belt's top surface, which is the outcome to avoid.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over-application shows up as silicone weeping from the belt edges, a slippery top
            surface, and a film on the floor around the machine. If that happens, wipe the excess
            from the belt top and the deck edges with a dry cloth and apply less next time. It is
            not damaging, just messy and slightly hazardous underfoot.
          </p>
          <p>
            After applying, walk on the belt at a slow speed for three to five minutes to
            distribute the lubricant evenly. Skipping this leaves it pooled where you put it, which
            is most of the value unrealised. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">step-by-step lubrication guide</a>
            covers the full procedure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How Often to Apply</h2>
<p>
            The useful interval is measured in running hours rather than calendar months, because a
            machine used daily and one used monthly wear at completely different rates.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Every 40 to 50 hours of use</strong> is the standard interval for a home
            treadmill. At three 45-minute sessions a week that is roughly every four months; at
            daily use it is closer to every six weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Every 30 hours</strong> if the user is significantly above average weight, if
            two or more people use the machine daily, or if it lives somewhere dusty like a garage.
            Load and contamination both accelerate the drying of the interface.
          </p>
          <p>
            The check that settles it is simple and worth doing between applications. Unplug the
            machine, reach under the belt near the centre and run a finger along the deck. It should
            feel slightly slick, like a lightly oiled surface. Dry, warm or gritty means apply now
            regardless of the calendar. Our
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication interval guide</a>
            covers the timing in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Signs You Have Left It Too Long</h2>
<p>
            Four symptoms indicate rising friction, and all of them mean lubricate before doing
            anything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt hesitates when you step on.</strong> The most reliable early signal.
            You step on and the belt momentarily slows before recovering, because the motor is
            having to overcome more resistance than it expects.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor sounds like it is working harder</strong>, particularly a change in
            pitch under load that was not there before.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The deck feels warm</strong> under the belt after a session. Some warmth is
            normal; noticeably hot is friction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A new noise appears</strong> — though noise has several possible sources and
            friction is only one, so diagnose rather than assume. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">noise guide</a>
            covers locating it, and
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the belt-slowing guide</a>
            covers the hesitation symptom specifically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What It Costs Per Year</h2>
<p>
            This is the cheapest maintenance in the category by a wide margin, which is what makes
            skipping it so expensive.
          </p>
          <p>
            A bottle of 100% silicone treadmill lubricant costs roughly $10 to $18 and contains
            enough for three to six applications. At the standard interval that is one to two years
            of coverage for a typically-used home machine, so the running cost is somewhere between
            $5 and $15 a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Against that: a treadmill motor replacement typically runs several hundred dollars with
            labour, and a controller board is comparable. Both are commonly caused by exactly the
            friction that lubrication prevents. On a mid-range machine, one avoided motor failure
            pays for four decades of lubricant.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            works through the whole annual figure, and
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            covers the repair-or-replace arithmetic when something does eventually fail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Not to Buy</h2>
<p>
            <strong>General-purpose silicone spray.</strong> Tempting and frequently wrong. Most
            contain solvents, propellants and additives alongside the silicone, and the silicone
            content is often low. Buy a product sold as treadmill lubricant.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anything petroleum-based.</strong> WD-40, motor oil, penetrating fluids. They
            attack the deck laminate and belt backing, and their residue collects grit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cooking oil.</strong> Oxidises into a sticky varnish that dramatically raises
            friction. Worse than doing nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wax on a standard deck, or silicone on a pre-waxed one.</strong> Both are
            wrong, and the second is the expensive one — a silicone-contaminated waxed deck is
            replaced rather than cleaned.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anything your manual does not endorse.</strong> Manufacturers specify lubricant
            type, and most state that other products void the deck, belt and motor warranty. That
            is enforceable — a service engineer assessing a motor failure will look at the deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="while-belt-loose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. While You Have the Belt Loose, Do These Too</h2>
<p>
            Lubricating means loosening the belt and getting access underneath, which is the
            awkward part of the job. Since you are already there and the machine is already
            unplugged, three other tasks cost almost nothing to add and cover most of the rest of
            the annual schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clean the deck edges and the belt underside properly.</strong> Not just where
            you are about to apply lubricant — the full width. Debris accumulates along the deck
            edges where the belt tracks, and it is the material that gets dragged into the contact
            area later.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the rollers by hand.</strong> With the belt slack, turn each roller
            slowly and feel for roughness or a catch. Smooth and silent is what you want; a gritty
            feel means bearings are wearing and you have advance warning of a repair rather than a
            surprise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the deck surface itself.</strong> A polished, shiny or grooved area
            under the belt means the low-friction layer is wearing through. Many decks can be
            flipped to their unused face, which effectively doubles their life — and knowing you
            need to is worth far more than discovering it after a motor failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then retension the belt properly rather than by feel. Our
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a>
            covers the lift test, and the
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring guide</a>
            covers tracking, which is the step most likely to need attention after the belt has
            been loosened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            For the great majority of home treadmills, buy a <strong>100% silicone treadmill
            lubricant in a squeeze bottle with a long applicator tube</strong>. It costs $10 to
            $18, lasts a year or more, goes exactly where it is needed, and is the correct product
            for a standard deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the manual first for deck type, without exception. Pre-waxed and self-lubricating
            decks are the one case where following this page's default advice would cause damage,
            and the check takes two minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then apply about an ounce along the centre third every 40 to 50 hours of use — 30 for
            heavier or multi-user households — and walk on it slowly for a few minutes afterwards
            to distribute it. That is the entire routine, it takes ten minutes, and it is the
            single highest-return thing you can do for a treadmill's lifespan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best lubricant for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                100% silicone treadmill lubricant, in a squeeze bottle with a long applicator tube. Silicone is inert toward the deck laminate, the belt backing and the adhesive between belt layers, and it stays slick rather than turning tacky and collecting grit. Check your manual first — pre-waxed decks must never receive silicone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my treadmill deck is pre-waxed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The manual will say, usually in the maintenance section. If you cannot find it, search your model number plus "lubrication" on the manufacturer's site or call their support line. Do not guess from brand or price — the same manufacturer often uses different deck types across its range, and applying silicone to a waxed deck means replacing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much lubricant should I use on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About one ounce — roughly two tablespoons — applied along the centre third of the deck under the belt, where almost all the contact happens. Then walk on it slowly for three to five minutes to distribute it. Over-application shows as silicone weeping from the belt edges and a slippery top surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular silicone spray on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. General-purpose silicone sprays typically contain solvents, propellants and additives alongside the silicone, and the silicone content is often low. Buy a product sold specifically as treadmill lubricant, which is formulated to be 100% silicone with no petroleum distillates.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a bottle of treadmill lubricant last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically three to six applications, which at the standard 40 to 50 hour interval is one to two years for a home machine used a few times a week. That works out at roughly $5 to $15 a year — against several hundred dollars for the motor replacement that skipping it eventually causes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I clean the deck before lubricating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and most people skip it. Old lubricant collects dust, carpet fibre and belt debris, and applying fresh silicone over that traps an abrasive slurry against the deck. Wipe the deck and belt underside with a lint-free cloth lightly dampened in isopropyl alcohol, and let it dry fully first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing and Applying Lubricant</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first of these is the only irreversible one on this page, and it is entirely avoidable with a two-minute check.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying silicone to a pre-waxed deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Silicone and the wax impregnated in these decks do not coexist, and the result is a contaminated surface that has to be replaced rather than cleaned. Read the manual before buying any product. The same manufacturer often uses different deck types across its range, so brand and price tell you nothing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying general-purpose silicone spray</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most contain solvents, propellants and additives alongside a relatively low silicone content, none of which belongs on a deck laminate or a belt backing. Products sold specifically as treadmill lubricant are formulated to be 100% silicone with no petroleum distillates. The price difference is a few dollars.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying an aerosol near the motor compartment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The motor, controller board and mains wiring sit directly under the front of the deck, and aerosol drift carries further than expected. If you must use a spray, unplug the machine, get the precision straw genuinely under the belt, and use short bursts well away from the front of the frame.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lubricating over old residue</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Months-old lubricant is full of dust, carpet fibre and belt debris. Fresh silicone applied on top traps that abrasive slurry directly against the deck, which is precisely the friction you were trying to remove. Clean with isopropyl alcohol on a lint-free cloth and let it dry fully first.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
