        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy <strong class="text-white">100% silicone treadmill lubricant in a bottle with an
            applicator tube</strong>. It costs $10 to $18, lasts a year or more, and puts the
            product where the contact actually happens rather than misting it across the motor
            compartment.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check your manual for deck type first — that is the one irreversible decision here, since
            silicone destroys a pre-waxed deck. Then apply about an ounce along the centre third
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">every 40 to 50 hours</a>
            and walk it in slowly.
          </p>
        </div>
